<p>
  Authenticates users against an LDAP directory and maps directory groups onto Nexus roles.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Bind mode.</strong> Leave <em>User DN Pattern</em> blank to use search bind: the plugin binds as the
  manager account, locates the user with the search filter, then binds as that user to verify the password. Set a
  pattern instead to build the user DN directly, which only works where the directory permits anonymous bind.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Group strategy.</strong> Directories expose membership incompatibly, so pick the one that matches yours:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>STATIC</strong> &mdash; the group entry lists its members. Costs one subtree search per lookup, so keep
    the group search base narrow.</li>
  <li><strong>DYNAMIC</strong> &mdash; the user entry lists its groups, normally in <code>memberOf</code>. Cheapest
    option. In OpenLDAP this attribute comes from an overlay that is off by default.</li>
  <li><strong>HIERARCHY</strong> &mdash; groups are the <code>ou</code> components of the user DN. No directory
    lookup, but moving a user between organizational units changes their groups.</li>
  <li><strong>NONE</strong> &mdash; authenticate only; roles are assigned to users individually in Nexus.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  <strong>Granting access.</strong> Directory groups arrive as role identifiers with source <code>LDAP</code>. Map
  them to Nexus privileges by creating roles whose ids match the group names, or by granting roles to individual
  users from the user administration screen.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Caching.</strong> Credentials are verified against the directory on every authentication and are never
  cached, so a password change or lockout takes effect immediately. Group lookups are cached, since every artifact
  request triggers one.
</p>
